{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.13.18","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"datasetVersion","sourceId":835452}],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Core Analysis Objective","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Understand how shoppers progress from viewing products to adding to cart and purchasing, pinpoint where the biggest drop-offs happen, and see how conversion varies across product categories, brands, and price tiers.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Specifically, we can frame this around 4 concrete questions:\n\n**Where do shoppers leak?** What percentage of sessions/users move from view → cart → purchase, and where is the steepest drop-off?\n\n**How do journeys behave in practice?** Do events always happen in view → cart → purchase order within a session, or do users buy without a recorded cart event, repeat views/carts, or span multiple sessions?\n\n**What drives higher or lower conversion?** Which category_code and brand segments convert best vs. struggle most?\n\n**How does price affect drop-off?** Do higher-priced items have steeper cart-to-purchase drop-offs than low- or mid-priced items?","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Importing Libraries\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd \nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\n\nimport pyarrow as pa\nimport pyarrow.parquet as pq\n\nimport kagglehub\nfrom kagglehub import KaggleDatasetAdapter\nimport zlib","metadata":{"_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-22T12:54:04.416988Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-22T12:54:04.418988Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-22T12:54:08.694961Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-22T12:54:04.41895Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-22T12:54:08.694056Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":1},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Listing all the files under the input folder\n\nimport os\nfor dirname, _, filenames in os.walk('/kaggle/input'):\n    for filename in filenames:\n        print(os.path.join(dirname, filename))\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-22T12:54:14.12832Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-22T12:54:14.128659Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-22T12:54:14.358468Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-22T12:54:14.128629Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-22T12:54:14.357506Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"/kaggle/input/datasets/mkechinov/ecommerce-behavior-data-from-multi-category-store/2019-Nov.csv\n/kaggle/input/datasets/mkechinov/ecommerce-behavior-data-from-multi-category-store/2019-Oct.csv\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":2},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Loading the Data ","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"We'll be initially loading 500,000 records rather that loading the entire dataset (which has almost 42M records). \n\nRather than randomly sampling individual records, sampling by user_id or user_session would allow me to preserve the relationships between events and understand the complete customer journey: \n\n**View → Add to Cart → Purchase.**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Since we have 9.24 million unique user session IDs, if we take 5% of that, it would give us approximately 462,000 sessions. \nReading 9.24 million user session IDs and randomly sampling 5% of that would waste a lot of time and RAM.\n\nThe workaround? -> Hashing the session IDs\n\nThe same session ID will always produce the same hash value.\n\n*So, how do we select the 5%?* \nWe use the below rule.\n\n```{python}\nhash_value % 20 == 0\n```\n\nThis means:\n- Calculate the hash value of a session ID.\n- Divide it by 20.\n- If the remainder is 0, keep that session.\n- Otherwise, discard it.\n\n*Why 20?* Because approximately 1 out of every 20 hash values will satisfy this condition.\n\n**That's approximately 5%!**\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"file_path = r\"/kaggle/input/datasets/mkechinov/ecommerce-behavior-data-from-multi-category-store/2019-Oct.csv\"\n\ncols_to_use = [\"user_id\",\n               \"event_time\",\n               \"event_type\", \n               \"brand\", \n               \"price\",\n               \"category_code\", \n               \"user_session\",\n               \"product_id\"]\nchunksize = 500_000\n# rng = np.random.default_rng(42)  # reproducible random sampling\n\nsample_rate = 0.05  # e.g., 5% of sessions\nthreshold = int(sample_rate * (2**32))","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def in_session_sample(session_series: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:\n    # Initial check to see whether the sample rate is between 0 and 1\n    if sample_rate <= 0:\n        return pd.Series(False, index=session_series.index)\n    if sample_rate >= 1:\n        return session_series.notna()\n\n    hashes = session_series.fillna(\"\").map( # Replaces missing session IDs with empty strings.\n        lambda s: zlib.crc32(s.encode(\"utf-8\")) # Converts the session ID into a numerical hash.\n        if s else 2**32\n    )\n    return hashes < threshold\n\n# def in_session_sample(session_series: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:\n#     hashes = session_series.fillna(\"\").map( # Replaces missing session IDs with empty strings.\n#         lambda s: zlib.crc32(s.encode(\"utf-8\")) # Converts the session ID into a numerical hash.\n#         if s else 2**32 \n#     )\n#     return hashes % 20 == 0 #Checks whether the hash is divisible by 20.\n\n\nreader = pd.read_csv(file_path, usecols=cols_to_use, chunksize=chunksize)\nwriter = None\n\n\n# For every chunk:\n# 1. Check whether the event is view, cart, or purchase.\n# 2. Check whether its session ID passes the 5% hash rule.\n# 3. Keep the row only if both conditions are satisfied.\n\nfor chunk in reader:\n    mask = (\n        chunk[\"event_type\"].isin([\"view\", \"cart\", \"purchase\"]) # core events in the funnel cycle\n        & in_session_sample(chunk[\"user_session\"]) # only focussing on the user session column for now\n    )\n    chunk = chunk[mask].copy()\n\n    if chunk.empty:\n        continue\n\n    # Parse the timestamps and assigning appropriate data types to columns\n    chunk[\"event_time\"] = pd.to_datetime(chunk[\"event_time\"], errors=\"coerce\") \n    chunk[\"event_type\"] = chunk[\"event_type\"].astype(\"category\")\n    if chunk[\"price\"].dtype == \"float64\":\n        chunk[\"price\"] = chunk[\"price\"].astype(\"float32\")\n\n    # Converts the current Pandas chunk into an Apache Arrow Table\n    # (the in-memory columnar format PyArrow uses to write Parquet files)\n    table = pa.Table.from_pandas(chunk, preserve_index=False)\n    if writer is None:\n        writer = pq.ParquetWriter(\"Oct_500K_sample.parquet\", table.schema, compression=\"snappy\")\n    writer.write_table(table)\n\nif writer is not None:\n    writer.close()\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-22T12:54:59.89682Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-22T12:54:59.897221Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-22T12:57:30.844734Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-22T12:54:59.89719Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-22T12:57:30.843678Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":5},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Data Cleaning and Preprocessing","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Understanding the Data","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"df = pd.read_parquet(\"Oct_500K_sample.parquet\")\ndf.head()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"202